#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a54e3eac-82c9-50e9-b900-7091b6f10844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313692} {:answer/id #uuid "7dec88e2-fde6-5491-b9b5-40b4e82e7217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313693} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7272c3-8422-5e70-89de-e66a3cdf649d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313694} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbb98b3-d2b1-5ecc-9064-52e74bbabd65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313695} {:answer/id #uuid "988b4525-731b-5e21-af0c-42a89c551fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bbb98b3-d2b1-5ecc-9064-52e74bbabd65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313695} :answer-field/id #uuid "f97c4742-5003-532f-8984-a74f6aa82ab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10527239-7a53-5ae0-a820-1a3f5dbaefa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123440" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {x}^{2n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{2n-n} \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236cae10-5a3d-597c-8842-341b21b83caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313699} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ff5f20-313c-54b4-8770-1e6cf917a637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313700} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bfc97f-ed1d-5a66-bcbf-48c8af2ba6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313701} {:answer/id #uuid "1aacad5d-9bc9-5954-b03e-f15b4a1471d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313702} {:answer/id #uuid "5355b126-9eaf-5fe4-bcb4-4a7af42ec1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ff5f20-313c-54b4-8770-1e6cf917a637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313700} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c816f61-b535-5f6d-bcac-ef38b7693a2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3498ccae-3b6f-52d0-a680-e89002f202e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141322" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=8{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThis given sequence is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges if \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "594f0477-ebf9-5099-b4d5-418bcafe7cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313706} {:answer/id #uuid "40e4f90a-9ace-5ed4-88bb-55f4b5312c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313707} {:answer/id #uuid "1b08e602-9acf-5d4b-90b0-4002ca59c6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313708} {:answer/id #uuid "18811060-62e7-54ba-bdb7-e6b7744025bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313709} {:answer/id #uuid "58562ea8-2102-5505-a2f0-cbd978b2b96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58562ea8-2102-5505-a2f0-cbd978b2b96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313710} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebc974a-217c-52db-8f75-ad777668b61e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a6ea49d-aab8-5fc3-8d1f-fbad3bc51925" :question/math-academy-id "q-141329" :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=2x{(\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}.$ \nSince ${x}^{4}$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{4}\\ge 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "981b5ffa-a0d4-5990-a936-459652f1649b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "981b5ffa-a0d4-5990-a936-459652f1649b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313713} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7665fa5-9555-5d76-96b9-b1d9f4ac714a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8225eb13-4cd4-5e81-ba79-8ee1fc4bff50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8225eb13-4cd4-5e81-ba79-8ee1fc4bff50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313715} :answer-field/id #uuid "d95d1ccd-88ba-51fb-ae00-5a93ba3739e9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaf84dc0-2718-5769-b160-3819e150d27e" :question/math-academy-id "q-347770" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n$x$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{5}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{5}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{5}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287aaa36-e215-586d-a825-dad38dff21cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313718} {:answer/id #uuid "09d323c2-25c2-56f4-9c20-a4714aafdd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313719} {:answer/id #uuid "51ab496c-1758-5e87-a707-094b00822798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313720} {:answer/id #uuid "bceaabf6-e2ac-5530-8e7e-d9e4bbdf8792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313721} {:answer/id #uuid "33c96734-2ad9-57d3-9e84-dda67bdd1b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d323c2-25c2-56f4-9c20-a4714aafdd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313719} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b6d9c9-1b64-5900-88bc-2e07371eacba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fc238f8-0118-5614-b187-86df412902ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-49570" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n+3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{n+3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{n}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{x}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{x}{3}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<x\\le 3.\n$$"}]}
